    {
      "cardId": "cmtibnhmy008q04ihgcxup05w",
      "term": "If you have WACC of 6%, acquirer’s WACC is 10% and a yield of 8%. Is it EPS accretive/dilutive (assuming you use the same capital structure blend as current company)? Does it create/destroy value?",
      "set": "M&A ",
      "definition": "Creates value (IRR > target’s WACC) but is EPS dilutive",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "Value creation is judged by comparing the deal's return to the target's own WACC.",
          "kind": "definition",
          "role": "framing"
        },
        {
          "text": "The target's 8% earnings yield exceeds its 6% WACC, so the deal earns two points above its risk-appropriate hurdle and creates value.",
          "kind": "causal",
          "role": "substance"
        },
        {
          "text": "The effective cost of funding the deal is the acquirer's 10% WACC.",
          "kind": "condition",
          "role": "substance"
        },
        {
          "text": "The 8% earnings yield is below the 10% funding cost.",
          "kind": "quantitative",
          "role": "substance"
        },
        {
          "text": "Because the earnings yield is below the funding cost, pro forma EPS falls.",
          "kind": "causal",
          "role": "substance"
        },
        {
          "text": "The deal is EPS dilutive.",
          "kind": "contrast",
          "role": "framing"
        },
        {
          "text": "The two tests can give different answers because value measures deal economics against the target's risk while EPS measures the accounting result against how the purchase is financed.",
          "kind": "contrast",
          "role": "substance"
        },
        {
          "text": "The deal creates value but is EPS dilutive.",
          "kind": "contrast",
